\title{The Corrected-Product Torsor of Normalized $q$-Series}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A Laurent series $f \in \CC((q))$ is \emph{normalized} if it has the shape
$f = q^{-1} + a_0 + a_1 q + a_2 q^2 + \cdots$, i.e.\ its polar part is exactly the monomial
$q^{-1}$ with coefficient $1$. Normalized series are the standard shape of Hauptmoduln and of
McKay--Thompson series, but they are not closed under multiplication: a product of $m$ normalized
series has a pole of order $m$. We study the unique monomial repair of this \emph{pole-order
obstruction}, the \emph{corrected product} $f \star g = qfg$. We prove that $\star$ is closed on
normalized series, that the correction exponent is unique, and that $\star$ makes the normalized
series a commutative group with identity $q^{-1}$ and inversion $f \mapsto q^{-2}f^{-1}$,
isomorphic to the group $1 + q\CC[[q]]$ of one-units via $f \mapsto qf$. We then determine the
invariant theory of the $\star$-orbits: a strictly descending separated filtration by depth
subgroups with all graded pieces isomorphic to $(\CC,+)$; a complete and free (but non-additive)
invariant system; torsion-freeness and unique divisibility, hence a $\QQ$-vector space structure,
with explicit faithful complex one-parameter subgroups obtained by substituting $qf-1$ into
$(1+X)^r$. The central computational result is an exact finite binomial expansion: for a $k$-deep
series the level-$m$ invariant of the $n$-th iterate is $\sum_{d \le \lfloor m/k\rfloor}
\binom{n}{d}\omega_d$ with weights independent of $n$; consequently every orbit invariant is a
polynomial in the iteration count of degree at most $\lfloor m/k\rfloor$, with leading term
$\binom{n}{j}c_k(f)^j$ at level $jk$, determined by $\lfloor m/k \rfloor + 1$ iterates, and the
depth is constant along non-trivial orbits. For the modular function
$J = q^{-1} + 196884q + 21493760q^2 + 864299970q^3 + \cdots$ the $q$-coefficient of $J^{\star n}$
is $196884\,n$ and the $q^3$-coefficient is $864299970\,n + \binom{n}{2}196884^2$.
\end{abstract}

